  "function": {
    "uniprot_function": "Constitutively active protein kinase that acts as a negative regulator in the hormonal control of glucose homeostasis, Wnt signaling and regulation of transcription factors and microtubules, by phosphorylating and inactivating glycogen synthase (GYS1 or GYS2), and other signaling proteins. Negatively regulates extrinsic apoptotic signaling pathway via death domain receptors. Essential component of the circadian clock. Phosphorylates translation initiation factor EIF2B and thereby plays a role in translational control. Regulates glycogen metabolism by phosphorylating glycogen synthase. In Wnt signaling, forms a multimeric complex with APC, AXIN1 and CTNNB1/beta-catenin and phosphorylates the N-terminus of CTNNB1 leading to its degradation mediated by ubiquitin/proteasomes. Plays an important role in ERBB2-dependent stabilization of microtubules at the cell cortex. Prevents CRMP2-dependent neuronal growth cone collapse by phosphorylating CRMP2. Phosphorylates BMAL1 and regulates circadian clock.",
    "catalytic_activity": "ATP + [tau protein] = ADP + [tau protein] phosphate (EC 2.7.11.26). ATP + [glycogen synthase] = ADP + [glycogen synthase] phosphate. Phosphorylates serine and threonine residues, preferentially with a priming phosphorylation at position P+4.",
    "subcellular_location": ["Cytoplasm", "Nucleus", "Cell membrane", "Plasma membrane"],
    "tissue_specificity": "Expressed in testis, thymus, prostate and ovary and weakly expressed in lung, brain and kidney. Elevated expression observed in skeletal muscle of type 2 diabetes patients.",
